{"reference":"Ezekiel 1","book":"Ezekiel","chapter":1,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"Reading the Opening Vision","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"Ezekiel 1 opens the book’s first major movement, chapters 1–3, with a dated first-person account: Ezekiel, a priest among the exiles by the Kebar canal, receives a vision and the word of YHWH. The description repeatedly uses terms such as “likeness” and “appearance” for the storm, creatures, wheels, expanse, throne, human-like figure, and radiance, presenting the vision through careful comparison. Across the received book, this first-person Ezekielian voice is sustained, while features such as collection, coordination, repetition, and textual development are also compatible with scribal shaping. That observation does not settle the book’s precise chronology or composition, but it helps readers distinguish the chapter’s narrated voice from questions about how the book reached its received form.","verse_refs":["Ezekiel 1:1-28","Ezekiel 1:1-3","Ezekiel 1:4-28"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_ezekiel_001_11866cf36e7c1c8ccc4dc425"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}